<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<pretext xml:lang="en-US">
  <docinfo>
    <directories external="../assets"/>
    <macros>
      \newcommand{\C}{\mathbb C}
      \newcommand{\Pone}{\mathbb P^1_{\C}}
      \newcommand{\cO}{\mathcal O}
      \DeclareMathOperator{\ord}{ord}
    </macros>
  </docinfo>
  <book xml:id="sample-note">
    <title>Sections and Global Generation on the Projective Line</title>
    <shorttitle>Projective line</shorttitle>
    <frontmatter xml:id="note-metadata">
      <bibinfo><author><personname>Jie Fu</personname></author></bibinfo>
      <titlepage><titlepage-items/></titlepage>
    </frontmatter>
    <chapter xml:id="fibres-and-orders">
      <title>Fibres, base ideals, and jets</title>
      <shorttitle>Fibres and jets</shorttitle>
      <p>Let <m>X</m> be a smooth connected complex projective curve. A line bundle <m>L</m> is a locally free sheaf of rank one: near each point, a local frame identifies its sections with regular functions. Write <m>H^0(X,L)</m> for the vector space of global sections. At a closed point <m>p</m>, the stalk <m>L_p</m> is a free module of rank one over the local ring <m>\cO_{X,p}</m>. Its fibre is the one-dimensional complex vector space <m>L(p)=L_p/\mathfrak m_pL_p</m>, where <m>\mathfrak m_p</m> is the maximal ideal.</p>
      <p>The bundle is <em>generated at</em> <m>p</m> if evaluation <m>H^0(X,L)\to L(p)</m> is surjective. Since the fibre has dimension one, this means that some global section is nonzero at <m>p</m>. The bundle is <em>globally generated</em> if this holds at every point.</p>
      <p>For a subspace <m>V\subset H^0(X,L)</m>, its <em>base ideal</em> is the image of the evaluation morphism</p>
      <md number="no">V\otimes_{\C}L^{-1}\longrightarrow\cO_X.</md>
      <p>In a local frame, this ideal is generated by the coefficient functions of sections in <m>V</m>. Changing the frame multiplies them by a common unit, so the ideal is independent of that choice. For the full section space, write <m>\mathfrak b(L)</m>. Global generation is equivalent to <m>\mathfrak b(L)=\cO_X</m>.</p>
      <p>A point is a base point of <m>V</m> precisely when all these coefficient functions vanish there. In the local ring, this says that the base ideal is contained in the maximal ideal. If one coefficient is nonzero at the point, that coefficient is a unit in the local ring, and the base ideal is the whole local ring. Applying this at every point proves the stated equivalence with global generation.</p>
      <p>If a nonzero section has local expression <m>s=fe</m> in a frame <m>e</m>, its order <m>\ord_p(s)</m> is the largest nonnegative integer <m>k</m> for which <m>f\in\mathfrak m_p^k</m>. This is independent of the frame. We assign the zero section order infinity. For an integer <m>r\geq0</m>, the <em>jet of order</em> <m>r</m> is the class of the germ in <m>L_p/\mathfrak m_p^{r+1}L_p</m>. With a local parameter <m>z</m>, a jet records the coefficients through <m>z^r</m>, including the constant term. Thus its vector space has dimension <m>r+1</m>.</p>
      <p>Smoothness makes the local ring a discrete valuation ring. Every nonzero germ can therefore be written as a power of a local parameter times a unit. The exponent is its order of vanishing. Although a change of parameter or frame changes the coefficients used to represent a jet, the quotient defining the jet is intrinsic. Surjectivity of the map to jets consequently does not depend on these choices.</p>
    </chapter>
    <chapter xml:id="two-charts">
      <title>Sections in two affine charts</title>
      <shorttitle>Two affine charts</shorttitle>
      <p>Write points of <m>\Pone</m> as <m>[X_0:X_1]</m>. The chart <m>U_0</m>, where <m>X_0\ne0</m>, has coordinate <m>t=X_1/X_0</m>. The chart <m>U_1</m>, where <m>X_1\ne0</m>, has coordinate <m>u=X_0/X_1</m>. On their intersection, <m>u=1/t</m>. For any integer <m>d</m>, the bundle <m>\cO_{\Pone}(d)</m> has frames <m>e_0</m> and <m>e_1</m> on these charts, with transition relation <m>e_1=t^de_0</m>.</p>
      <proposition xml:id="sections-description">
        <statement>
          <p>If <m>d\geq0</m>, global sections of <m>\cO_{\Pone}(d)</m> are identified with homogeneous polynomials of degree <m>d</m> in <m>X_0,X_1</m>. Their vector space has dimension <m>d+1</m>. If <m>d\lt0</m>, there are no nonzero global sections.</p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>A section on <m>U_0</m> is <m>f(t)e_0</m> with <m>f</m> a polynomial, and a section on <m>U_1</m> is <m>g(u)e_1</m> with <m>g</m> a polynomial. They glue precisely when</p>
          <md number="no">g(u)=u^df(1/u).</md>
          <p>If <m>f(t)=\sum_i a_it^i</m>, the right side is <m>\sum_i a_iu^{d-i}</m>. Its exponents are distinct, so it is a polynomial exactly when every nonzero term satisfies <m>i\leq d</m>. For negative <m>d</m>, this forces <m>f=0</m>. For nonnegative <m>d</m>, it says that <m>f</m> has degree at most <m>d</m>. The corresponding homogeneous polynomial is</p>
          <md number="no">F(X_0,X_1)=\sum_{i=0}^d a_iX_0^{d-i}X_1^i.</md>
          <p>Conversely, dehomogenizing this polynomial gives <m>f(t)=F(1,t)</m> and <m>g(u)=F(u,1)</m>, which satisfy the gluing relation. The displayed monomials form a basis, proving the dimension assertion.</p>
        </proof>
      </proposition>
      <p>A homogeneous polynomial defines a section through its two dehomogenizations. Rescaling homogeneous coordinates multiplies its value by the corresponding power of the scale. The two local coefficient functions above account for this transformation through the transition relation. In particular, whether the section vanishes at a point is independent of the chosen homogeneous coordinates.</p>
    </chapter>
    <chapter xml:id="global-generation">
      <title>Global generation</title>
      <proposition xml:id="generation-criterion">
        <statement>
          <p>The bundle <m>\cO_{\Pone}(d)</m> is globally generated if and only if <m>d\geq0</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>For <m>d\geq0</m>, the section represented by <m>X_0^d</m> has coefficient one in the frame <m>e_0</m>, so it is nonzero everywhere on <m>U_0</m>. The section represented by <m>X_1^d</m> is similarly nonzero on <m>U_1</m>. These charts cover the projective line. For <m>d=0</m>, both sections are the constant section one. For negative <m>d</m>, <xref ref="sections-description"/> gives a zero section space, which cannot surject onto any fibre.</p>
        </proof>
      </proposition>
      <p>For <m>d=1</m>, the two sections <m>X_0,X_1</m> generate the bundle, although each vanishes at one point. If we use only the subspace spanned by <m>X_0</m>, its base ideal is the unit ideal on <m>U_0</m> and the ideal <m>(u)</m> on <m>U_1</m>. Its unique base point is <m>[0:1]</m>. This distinguishes generation by the full section space from generation by a chosen subspace.</p>
    </chapter>
    <chapter xml:id="jet-surjectivity">
      <title>Prescribing jets at a point</title>
      <shorttitle>Prescribing jets</shorttitle>
      <proposition xml:id="jet-criterion">
        <statement>
          <p>Let <m>p\in\Pone</m>, and let <m>r</m> be a nonnegative integer. The map from global sections of <m>\cO_{\Pone}(d)</m> to their jets of order <m>r</m> at <m>p</m> is surjective if and only if <m>d\geq r</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>First suppose <m>p=[1:a]</m>, and put <m>z=t-a</m>. Every jet, expressed in the frame <m>e_0</m>, has a unique representative <m>\sum_{i=0}^r c_iz^i</m>. Indeed, reduction modulo <m>z^{r+1}</m> turns a regular germ into its truncated polynomial; a denominator nonzero at <m>p</m> is invertible in this quotient. If <m>d\geq r</m>, the homogeneous polynomial</p>
          <md number="no">F(X_0,X_1)=\sum_{i=0}^r c_i(X_1-aX_0)^iX_0^{d-i}</md>
          <p>has degree <m>d</m> and dehomogenizes to exactly that representative. It therefore defines a section realizing the prescribed jet.</p>
          <p>The remaining point is <m>p=[0:1]</m>. Here <m>u</m> is a local parameter and <m>e_1</m> is a frame. The polynomial <m>\sum_{i=0}^r c_iX_0^iX_1^{d-i}</m> realizes the jet <m>\sum_{i=0}^r c_iu^i</m>. Thus every point is covered. If <m>d\lt r</m>, the source has dimension less than <m>r+1</m> by <xref ref="sections-description"/>, while the target has dimension <m>r+1</m>. Surjectivity is impossible.</p>
        </proof>
      </proposition>
      <p>For example, a section of <m>\cO_{\Pone}(1)</m> can prescribe both the constant and linear coefficients at one point. On the first chart, the polynomial <m>c_0+c_1(t-a)</m> realizes these two values. Prescribing a quadratic coefficient as well would require a jet space of dimension three, while the section space still has dimension two. This is why the order of the prescribed jet matters even for a globally generated bundle.</p>
      <p>For <m>r=0</m>, <xref ref="jet-criterion"/> recovers <xref ref="generation-criterion"/>. For <m>r=d\geq0</m>, the jet map is an isomorphism. A jet is zero precisely when the section vanishes to order at least <m>r+1</m>, so its kernel describes the sections with that prescribed vanishing.</p>
    </chapter>
  </book>
</pretext>
